WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:02.390
Your agent works for a week.

2
00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:04.309
Then it falls apart.

3
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:05.830
The wrong account.

4
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.730
It forgets what it learned.

5
00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.210
And nobody can say what it changed.

6
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:13.460
PassionCode.ai is the agent-agnostic

7
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.290
operating system for AI-native teams:

8
00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:20.390
a workplace where agents do the work,
and you see all of it.

9
00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.470
It runs on your machines, as open source,

10
00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:26.370
with the agents and
subscriptions you already have.

11
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.010
Their work leaves a record you can open.

12
00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:34.430
For you, it starts with one command:
skills for Claude Code or Codex.

13
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:36.550
No account, no key.

14
00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.170
Fabric, in preview, is the home
for your projects and their agents.

15
00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:44.990
Your coding agent shows its
plan before it changes anything.

16
00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:49.690
Switchboard: each agent on the
account it should, limits in view.

17
00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:54.230
Dashboards: state,
spend and control, in one window.

18
00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:57.770
Observatory: what changed, with the evidence.

19
00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.790
Inbox, in preview:

20
00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:03.170
mail your agents answer
only within your rules.

21
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.490
Agents in one project share its board,
memory and hand-offs,

22
00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:09.870
so one picks up where another stopped.

23
00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:14.460
For an organization, we bring agents
onto your machines, connect them,

24
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.410
and show what each one
cost and what it delivered.

25
00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.610
Agents are easy to start and hard to keep.

26
00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:23.110
We are building what holds them together.
